    "BASIC SENTENCE STRUCTURE: Subject + Verb + Object (SVO)\n• Subject: Who/what performs the action.\n• Verb: The action or state.\n• Object: Receives the action.\nExample: Emeka (S) reads (V) a book (O).\n\nTYPES BY STRUCTURE\n1. Simple: One independent clause — 'The boy ran.'\n2. Compound: Two independent clauses + coordinating conjunction — 'The boy ran and the girl walked.'\n3. Complex: Independent + dependent clause — 'The boy ran because he was late.'\n\nTYPES BY PURPOSE\n1. Declarative: Statement — 'She is a student.'\n2. Interrogative: Question — 'Is she a student?'\n3. Imperative: Command — 'Open the door.'\n4. Exclamatory: Strong emotion — 'What a wonderful day!'",
